INT. FLOODED PARISH CHURCH - BELFRY STAIRCASE - NIGHT

A narrow stone stair spiraling up inside the bell tower. Black water laps the lower steps, thick with floating hymnals. A single candle burns in a niche. Wind moans through the louvers above.

FATHER AUGUSTINE BELLWEATHER, 60s, in a soaked cassock, climbs backward up the stairs, a crucifix held out like a shield. His breath comes in small, terrified gasps.

Below him, in the water, something rises: BARTHOLOMEW "BARTY" GRAVES, 40s, the sexton, drowned-pale, eyes open, his coat streaming. He climbs the stairs hand over hand, unhurried, smiling.

FATHER BELLWEATHER
Barty. Stay where you are.

BARTY
Father. It's cold down here.

FATHER BELLWEATHER
I know. I know it is.

BARTY
You left me.

FATHER BELLWEATHER
I went for help.

BARTY
You went for the key.

The priest stops. His heel finds the next stair. He grips the rail.

FATHER BELLWEATHER
The bell is not for you, Barty.

BARTY
Then who is it for?

FATHER BELLWEATHER
It is for all of us.

BARTY
(softly)
Ring it.

A telephone rings. Somewhere high in the belfry, an old black rotary set, impossible, plugged into nothing. It rings and rings.

BARTY (V.O.)
(from the phone, distant)
Father Bellweather, please pick up.

FATHER BELLWEATHER
(whispering)
God have mercy.

BARTY
That is not me.

BARTY (V.O.)
(from the phone)
Father, please. I'm waiting.

The priest turns his face toward the sound. Barty reaches a pale hand to his ankle. The candle gutters. Water rises another step.

FATHER BELLWEATHER
(very quietly)
Then let me answer.

He tears free, lunges up the last turn of the stairs, and the dark closes behind him like a door.
